{
  "summary": "Rewrites the `torcontrol` fuzz target to drive a real `TorControlConnection` over a mocked `DynSock` socket connected to a simulated Tor daemon instead of directly invoking controller callbacks. The fake daemon answers handshake commands with structured wire-format replies for PROTOCOLINFO, SAFECOOKIE authentication, GETINFO, and ADD_ONION, while asserting invariant properties such as proper nonce generation, port mapping, and unescaped password delivery. The PR also fixes a bug in the `DynSock::Wait()` test utility where socket instances inadvertently deleted themselves.",
  "problem": "The existing `torcontrol` fuzz target passed arbitrary strings capped at 16 characters directly to internal callbacks, leaving wire-level parsing (`ProcessBuffer()`, `ReceiveAndProcess()`) untested and making success paths impossible to reach without valid cryptographic responses. Additionally, `DynSock::Wait()` was broken for callers because it took owning shared ownership of `this`.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author reports line coverage on `src/torcontrol.cpp` increases from 52% on master to 81% after a 10-minute local run (PR description).",
    "Author notes the added password check finds the escaping bug previously fixed in #36637 within a few hundred runs (commit cbd8dd443b)."
  ],
